# function under(x) { return x - 1.5; }   function twice(x) { return 2 * x; }
# Beside one proven number the other side is an int32 wherever such code runs, or else a double: it is asked for the one, then for the other, and the operator works on two doubles in place.
# The stub that takes the number raw stands in a rare block, for what is no number. It was called at every turn: ten instructions within it, and the moves in front.
# RUN: emit f1:Body f2:Body mechanisms=none+TypedOperations+UnboxedNumbers
# CHECK-LABEL: unit f1:Body
# CHECK: = AboveEqual(
# CHECK-NEXT: = Branch({{*}}, unlikely #
# CHECK: = IToD(
# CHECK-NEXT: = Sub(
# CHECK: = Branch({{*}}, rare #
# CHECK: = BitwiseCast(
